        "titulo": "The fleeting vowel: the secret of the feminine and neuter plural",
        "explicacao": "The fleeting vowel: the secret of the feminine and neuter plural. This is one of the rules that confuse learners most, yet it is predictable once you see the logic. When the stem ends in two consonants, a vowel (о or е) is inserted between them in the genitive plural to make the word easier to say. The logic: Russian dislikes hard consonant clusters at the end of a word, so it adds a vowel. English does the same thing when it says \"film\" as \"fil-um\" or \"athlete\" as \"ath-a-lete\". Examples with о and with е follow below. A practical rule: before the last consonant, after ж, ч, ш, щ (and after ь or й) you usually get -ЕК (кошек, девочек); after other consonants you usually get -ОК (палок, окон). Careful: there are exceptions. If in doubt, check a dictionary. The most common words you will end up memorising. Examples in sentences:",
